{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 24, "model_name": "meta/llama-3.1-8b-instruct", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was a member of the House of Raymond and the ruler of the County of Tripoli from 1152 until his death in 1187. He was the son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli and Hodierna of Jerusalem.\n\nRaymond III Count of Tripoli was married to Eschiva of Bures and together they had at least three children. Their marriage was arranged as part of a treaty between the County of Tripoli and the Kingdom of Jerusalem.\n\n* **Early Life and Accession** *\nRaymond III Count of Tripoli succeeded his father Raymond II Count of Tripoli in 1152. He was still a minor at the time and his mother Hodierna of Jerusalem served as regent until 1158.\n\nDuring his early years as Count of Tripoli Raymond III Count of Tripoli was involved in several conflicts with the neighboring Muslim states. He participated in the Siege of Ascalon in 1153 but was not present at the Siege of Shaizar in 1150. Raymond III Count of Tripoli also participated in the Battle of Montgisard in 1177 and the Battle of Marj Ayyun in 1179.\n\n* **Relationship with the Kingdom of Jerusalem** *\nRaymond III Count of Tripoli had a complex relationship with the Kingdom of Jerusalem. He was a vassal of the Kingdom of Jerusalem but often acted independently. He was involved in several conflicts with the Kingdom of Jerusalem including the conflict over the County of Tripoli's border with the Kingdom of Jerusalem.\n\n* **Relationship with the Muslim States** *\nRaymond III Count of Tripoli had a complex relationship with the Muslim states that bordered the County of Tripoli. He was involved in several conflicts with the Muslim states including the Battle of Ain Jalut in 1179. Raymond III Count of Tripoli also participated in the Siege of Darb Saliha in 1180.\n\nRaymond III Count of Tripoli died in 1187 and was succeeded by his son Bohemond IV.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.014